# Behavioral Design Specification: Ibex Core Top-Level Module

## 1. Scope and Functional Overview

This specification defines the externally observable behavior of the top-level module of the Ibex 32-bit RISC-V processor core. The module is a configurable, in-order RISC-V core that fetches, decodes, executes, and retires instructions. It provides an instruction-side memory interface, a data-side memory interface, interrupt inputs, debug request handling, configuration/status registers, and optional performance-monitoring and security-related features.

The core must comply with the RISC-V Privileged Specification version 1.11 for the implemented feature set. It supports Machine Mode and, when configured, User Mode operation. The implemented base ISA is RV32I, with optional support for the M extension, the B extension, compressed instructions, and control/status register instructions.

## 2. Configuration Behavior

The behavior of the core is controlled by its configuration parameters. The core must implement only the features selected by the configuration and must behave appropriately for unsupported or disabled features.

- When the integer register file is configured for the RV32E variant, only 16 architectural registers must be available. Otherwise, the full 32-register RV32I register file must be provided.
- The M extension must provide multiply and divide instructions when enabled.
- The B extension must provide the implemented bit-manipulation instructions when enabled.
- The multiplier implementation setting selects the performance/area trade-off for multiply operations. This affects cycle timing but must not change architectural results.
- When a third writeback pipeline stage is enabled, instruction retirement timing and load/store writeback behavior change. Architectural results must remain correct.
- The instruction cache option, when enabled, allows instruction fetches to be served from an internal cache. The cache must be invalidated when architecturally required. If cache ECC is enabled, detected error conditions must be handled as instruction fetch faults.
- The physical memory protection option, when enabled, adds PMP address-checking behavior for instruction and data accesses.
- The debug trigger option, when enabled, adds address-match trigger functionality used to enter Debug Mode.
- The secure execution option, when enabled, adds data-independent timing and dummy-instruction features controlled through a custom control register.
- The debug halt address and debug exception address parameters determine where the core jumps when entering Debug Mode or when an exception occurs in Debug Mode.
- The number and width of optional performance-monitoring event counters are configuration-controlled.

## 3. Reset and Start-Up

On reset, the startup-enable state shall be cleared. Until `fetch_enable_i` has been sampled high using the ungated input clock, the internally gated core clock shall remain disabled and the core shall not begin instruction fetching or execution. A single-cycle assertion of `fetch_enable_i` shall be sufficient to enable startup. Once sampled high, the startup-enable state shall remain set until the next reset, regardless of subsequent values of `fetch_enable_i`.

After reset and after being enabled, the core must start fetching from the reset vector. The reset vector must be derived from the supplied boot address by replacing the least significant byte of that boot address with the value `0x80`. The trap-vector base address must be initialized to the supplied boot address.

## 4. Clocking and Sleep Behavior

The core may gate its own internal clock when idle. A test-enable input must force the clock-gating circuitry to be transparent during testing.

Except when `test_en_i` bypasses the clock gate, the normal internal clock enable shall be qualified by the latched startup-enable state. Before startup has been enabled, controller busy, debug request, pending interrupt, and pending NMI conditions shall not enable the internal core clock. After startup has been enabled, any of these activity or wake-up conditions may enable the internal clock.

The core must enter a low-power/sleep state when architectural execution reaches a wait-for-interrupt condition and there are no outstanding instruction-side or data-side memory transactions. The sleep output must be asserted in this state and deasserted when the core wakes.

The core must wake from the sleep state when any of the following occurs:

- a pending, enabled, maskable interrupt is asserted;
- a non-maskable interrupt is asserted;
- a debug request is asserted.

The sleep behavior must also account for the fact that once fetch-enable has been asserted, a debug request or pending interrupt must wake the core even if the core is otherwise idle.

## 5. Instruction Fetch Behavior

The core must fetch instructions through its instruction-side memory interface. The interface uses a request/accept/response protocol: the core presents a fetch request, the memory side may accept it, and a later response provides the instruction data or an error indication.

The core must support both 16-bit compressed instructions and 32-bit uncompressed instructions. The fetched instruction stream may contain a mix of both. If a compressed instruction is fetched, it must be transparently expanded before execution.

When the instruction memory system reports an error for a fetch, the core must take an instruction access fault exception. If an instruction fetch is denied by physical memory protection, the core must not issue the fetch request to the external instruction interface and must instead raise an instruction access fault.

The core must redirect instruction fetching on:

- taken branches and jumps;
- exception entry;
- interrupt entry;
- return-from-exception instructions;
- return-from-debug instructions;
- debug entry.

When a redirect occurs, instructions fetched before the redirect but not yet executed must not be allowed to retire.

The core must also implement the architectural `FENCE.I` behavior. After `FENCE.I`, subsequent instruction fetches must observe previously performed instruction-memory writes. If an instruction cache is present, it must be invalidated so that stale cached instructions are not used.

## 6. Instruction Execution and Data Memory Behavior

The core must execute instructions in program order. Multi-cycle instructions may stall the progress of later instructions until they complete. An instruction is retired only when its architectural effects, such as register file writes and CSR updates, are committed.

The data-side memory interface must support word, half-word, and byte loads and stores. Loads must support both signed and unsigned extension according to the instruction. Stores must drive the appropriate byte strobes and write data.

Each data access is presented to the data memory interface with the following information as appropriate:

- whether the access is a read or write;
- the byte address;
- which bytes are accessed;
- the write data;
- an indication of whether the response is data or an error.

The core must handle misaligned loads and stores by performing the access as multiple aligned memory transactions. If one transaction of a misaligned access receives an error response, the core must still issue any remaining transaction(s) for that access, ignore their responses, and then raise the corresponding load or store access fault exception.

If a data access is denied by physical memory protection, the core must not issue the request to the external data interface and must instead raise a load or store access fault exception as appropriate.

If the data memory system reports an error for a load or store, the core must raise a load access fault or store access fault exception. The trap value for such an exception must contain the address of the access that failed. For a misaligned access, the trap value must contain the address of the missing portion of the access.

## 7. Exceptions and Interrupts

The core must implement trap handling according to the RISC-V Privileged Specification. The core must support the following exception causes:

- instruction access fault;
- illegal instruction;
- breakpoint;
- load access fault;
- store access fault;
- environment call from User Mode;
- environment call from Machine Mode.

It must also support the following interrupt sources:

- non-maskable interrupt;
- fast/local interrupts;
- external interrupt;
- timer interrupt;
- software interrupt.

Maskable interrupts must be controlled by the global interrupt-enable bit in the machine status register and by the individual interrupt-enable bits in the machine interrupt-enable register. An interrupt is pending only when its corresponding interrupt input is asserted and its enable bit is set. The interrupt-pending register must reflect the pending and enabled interrupt state.

The core must prioritize simultaneously pending interrupts according to the RISC-V Privileged Specification. The non-maskable interrupt has the highest priority. Among the maskable interrupts, higher interrupt IDs generally have higher priority, except that timer interrupt priority is lower than software interrupt priority.

When an exception or interrupt is taken, the core must:

- save the architectural program counter to the machine exception program counter;
- write the exception cause to the machine cause register;
- write the trap value to the machine trap value register where applicable;
- save the current interrupt-enable state into the previous-interrupt-enable field;
- clear the global interrupt-enable bit;
- set the previous-privilege field to the current privilege mode;
- jump to the appropriate trap handler address.

For exceptions, the trap handler address must be the base address in the machine trap-vector register. For interrupts, the core must use vectored mode: the trap handler address must be the base address plus four times the interrupt ID. The non-maskable interrupt has ID 31, so its handler address is the base plus `0x7C`.

The non-maskable interrupt must be enabled regardless of the global interrupt-enable and individual interrupt-enable settings. During non-maskable interrupt handling, all interrupts, including a second non-maskable interrupt, must be ignored. Nested non-maskable interrupts are not supported.

All interrupt inputs must be treated as level-sensitive. An interrupt remains pending while asserted and enabled; the external source must deassert it once handled.

On execution of an MRET instruction, the core must:

- restore the machine privilege mode from the saved previous-privilege field;
- restore the global interrupt-enable state from the saved previous-interrupt-enable field;
- jump to the address in the machine exception program counter.

On execution of a DRET instruction, the core must exit Debug Mode and jump to the debug program counter.

Hardware nesting of interrupts/exceptions is not supported. The core must automatically disable interrupts when entering a trap handler. Software may re-enable interrupts within the handler if desired.

## 8. Privilege Modes and Control/Status Registers

The core must support Machine Mode and User Mode. After reset, the core must be in Machine Mode. All interrupts and exceptions must be handled in Machine Mode. The machine status register must record the privilege mode saved on trap entry and restore it on MRET.

The core must implement the following categories of control/status registers:

- machine status and ISA registers;
- machine interrupt-enable and interrupt-pending registers;
- machine trap-vector, exception program counter, cause, and trap value registers;
- machine scratch register;
- performance-counter and event-selector registers;
- physical memory protection configuration and address registers;
- debug control/status and debug program counter registers;
- debug trigger registers when debug trigger support is enabled;
- custom CPU control and security seed registers when the corresponding features are enabled;
- hardware thread ID register.

CSR accesses must obey the required privilege level. An access to a CSR that does not exist, is unavailable in the current privilege mode, is read-only when written, or is not writable must cause an illegal instruction exception.

Fields that are architecturally read-only or unsupported must ignore writes and read as their constant values. Writes to read-only registers must not change the register state.

The machine ISA register must reflect the implemented ISA configuration. Writes to it must not change its value.

The machine trap-vector register must be aligned to 256 bytes. Its mode field must read as a constant indicating vectored interrupt mode.

The hardware thread ID register must return the configured hart ID value.

The custom CPU control register, when security or instruction-cache features are enabled, must control the corresponding runtime features. If a referenced feature is not configured, the associated control field must read as zero and writes must have no effect.

The custom security seed register must not be readable; reads must return zero. Writes must reseed the security-related pseudo-random generators used by optional security features.

User Mode `time` registers are not implemented. Accesses to those registers must trap as illegal instruction exceptions.

## 9. Physical Memory Protection

When physical memory protection is enabled, the core must check every instruction fetch, load, and store against the programmed PMP regions. The number of regions, address matching granularity, access permissions, and locking behavior must follow the RISC-V Privileged Specification.

An instruction fetch that fails the PMP check must not be presented on the instruction memory interface and must cause an instruction access fault. A load or store that fails the PMP check must not be presented on the data memory interface and must cause the appropriate load or store access fault.

PMP configuration and address registers must be implemented as described by the RISC-V Privileged Specification. The core must interpret the PMP configuration fields according to that specification, including the reserved write-without-read encoding.

## 10. Debug Behavior

The core must support execution-based debug in accordance with the RISC-V Debug Specification. A debug request input must cause the core to enter Debug Mode.

When the core enters Debug Mode, it must:

- save the address of the next instruction that would have been executed into the debug program counter register;
- record the reason for entering Debug Mode in the debug control/status register;
- jump to the configured debug halt address.

Supported debug entry causes include:

- halting debug request;
- single-step;
- instruction address trigger match, when debug triggers are enabled;
- EBREAK instruction behavior configured to enter Debug Mode.

In Debug Mode, all interrupts, including the non-maskable interrupt, must be ignored. Debug-mode-specific CSRs must be accessible only while in Debug Mode. An attempt to access them outside Debug Mode must cause an illegal instruction exception.

If an exception occurs while in Debug Mode, the core must jump to the configured debug exception address instead of the normal trap handler address.

When the debugger modifies the debug program counter register, the core must use the modified value as the resumption address on DRET.

When single-step is enabled, the core must execute exactly one instruction and then re-enter Debug Mode.

When address-match debug triggers are enabled, a match on the configured instruction address must cause Debug Mode entry.

## 11. Performance Counters

The core must implement `mcycle` and `minstret` as 64-bit counters. `mcycle` must count clock cycles. `minstret` must count retired instructions.

The core may implement additional performance-monitoring event counters based on the configured number of counters. When an event counter is not implemented, reads from its registers must return zero.

The following events must be available for counting when the corresponding event counters are implemented:

- number of cycles;
- number of retired instructions;
- number of cycles waiting for data memory;
- number of cycles waiting for instruction fetch;
- number of loads;
- number of stores;
- number of unconditional jumps;
- number of conditional branches;
- number of taken conditional branches;
- number of retired compressed instructions;
- number of cycles waiting for multiply completion;
- number of cycles waiting for divide completion.

The `mcountinhibit` register must allow software to disable the cycle counter, the retired-instruction counter, and each implemented event counter individually.

Event-selector registers must be implemented for the implemented event counters. Each such register must be hardwired to select the event associated with that counter.

## 12. Security-Related Behavior

When the secure execution feature is enabled, the core must support:

- data-independent timing, so that certain instruction execution times do not depend on operand data values;
- dummy instruction insertion, so that architecturally invisible dummy instructions may be executed to obscure the core’s behavior.

These features must be controlled through the custom CPU control register. The dummy-instruction insertion frequency must be controllable, and the core must support reseeding of the pseudorandom dummy-instruction sequence through writes to the security seed register.

When the secure execution feature is not enabled, these control fields must read as zero and the features must not affect normal execution.

## 13. RISC-V Formal Interface

When the RISC-V Formal Interface is enabled at compile time, the core must provide a formal observation output stream.

For every retired instruction, the formal interface must present one transaction containing:

- a valid indication;
- a monotonically increasing unique order identifier;
- the instruction word;
- whether the instruction trapped or halted;
- whether the instruction was part of an interrupt handler;
- the privilege mode and instruction length mode;
- source register addresses and read data;
- destination register address and write data;
- the program counter before and after the instruction;
- memory access address, read/write masks, and read/write data when the instruction performs a memory access.

The formal interface must be strictly observational and must not change the architectural behavior of the core.

## 14. Summary of Required Behavior

In summary, the core must:

- implement a configurable RV32 RISC-V processor that fetches and retires instructions in order;
- provide instruction-side and data-side memory interfaces with support for error responses and valid/accept/response timing;
- handle compressed instructions, misaligned data accesses, and multi-cycle instructions correctly;
- implement RISC-V exceptions and vectored interrupt handling with the required priority and CSR side effects;
- support Machine Mode and, when configured, User Mode privilege operation;
- provide the required set of CSRs, physical memory protection, debug, and performance counter functionality;
- support optional security features and a RISC-V formal interface without affecting architectural correctness.
